{"name":"Forged Iron","hex":"#555257","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"forged","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Fake (as documents); falsified.","Fabricated by forging or at a forge, by working hot metal"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of forge. To force forward against opposition."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forged"},{"term":"iron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, inexpensive metal, silvery grey when untarnished, that rusts, is attracted by magnets, and is used in making steel: a chemical element having atomic number 26 and symbol Fe.","Any material, not a steel, predominantly made of elemental iron."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the metal iron.","Strong , inflexible."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Forging","blocks":[{"text":"Forging is a manufacturing process involving the shaping of metal using localized compressive forces. The blows are delivered with a hammer or a die. Forging is often classified according to the temperature at which it is performed: cold forging, warm forging, or hot forging. For the latter two, the metal is heated, usually in a forge. Forged parts can range in weight from less than a kilogram to hundreds of metric tons. Forging has been done by smiths for millennia; the traditional products were kitchenware, hardware, hand tools, edged weapons, cymbals, and jewellery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Bochumer_Verein-03-50142.jpg/330px-Bochumer_Verein-03-50142.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forging"},{"title":"Iron","blocks":[{"text":"Iron is a chemical element; it has symbol Fe and atomic number 26. It is a metal that belongs to the first transition series and group 8 of the periodic table. It is, by mass, the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most abundant element in the Earth's crust. In its metallic state it was mainly deposited by meteorites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Walzen_Stab.jpg/500px-Walzen_Stab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Walzen_Stab.jpg/500px-Walzen_Stab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A billet in an open-die forging press","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalzen_Stab.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg/500px-ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg/1280px-ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg/500px-ForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A cross-section of a forged connecting rod that has been etched to show the grain flow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForgedConrodShowingEtchedSection-s.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Bochumer_Verein-08-50124.jpg/500px-Bochumer_Verein-08-50124.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Bochumer_Verein